Original commit body from 0973c09fa245922860e16b42378ed7594dc1bbcb:

The per-PR gate decided "should pytest run?" from the set of changed
packages/*/src/**.py files. Anything else produced an empty source set, so it
printed "No Python source files changed" and exited 0 — and the pipeline went
GREEN having run nothing.

PR CLASSES THAT RAN NO TESTS AND REPORTED GREEN:
  - test-only changes (every test fix, including a fix TO a broken test)
  - scripts/*.py            (the whole CLI/tooling tree, incl. auto-pr)
  - .agent/hooks/**.py      (agent infrastructure)
  - any mix of the above with docs or CI YAML

Caught on the WI-fodad PR, whose committed manifest carried 0 changed source
files and 1 selected test: smart-test had correctly selected the changed test
and CI threw the answer away. That PR — a fix for a flaky test — never ran the
test it fixes.

This is L48's family one level up. The recorded lesson is "a test that cannot be
SELECTED is indistinguishable from a test that passes"; this was an entire CLASS
OF PULL REQUEST for which the suite never ran, reported as a pass.

FIX: skip only when the manifest's SELECTED_TESTS section is empty — the
question the gate actually means to ask. Changed-source files still size the
COVERAGE target, which legitimately needs them; the two concerns were conflated
in one condition and are now separate.

PINNED BY TESTS THAT EXECUTE THE GATE RATHER THAN READ IT. The shell block is
extracted from the YAML and RUN against a fabricated manifest with stub binaries
on a REPLACED PATH (L49), asserting on whether pytest was actually invoked. A
source-text assertion would have been worthless here: the old broken condition
also mentioned the manifest, so "the string SELECTED_TESTS appears near the
skip" would have passed while the behaviour was broken.

MUTATION-TESTED: reintroducing the original condition turns 3 of the 4 tests
red; restoring the fix turns them green. Two harness bugs were caught this way
before shipping (a printf '%s' with a Python repr wrote a literal backslash-n so
the diff never matched, and a docs-only fixture exercised the earlier skip
rather than the one under test), and a `set -e` hazard on the now-reachable
empty-COV_PATHS line was checked directly and found harmless.

The new test is added to KNOWN_UNREACHABLE with its consequence stated: its
subject is a YAML file, so no Python source maps to it and an edit to that
workflow does not select it per-PR. The gate that detects unselectable tests is
itself unselectable when a test is added — the same "who guards the guard" gap
that list keeps recording.

